	(text_box "Using the CP2102N GPIO pins directly to drive `NRST` and `BOOT0` eliminates external active components like transistors or logic inverters entirely.\n\nBy default, standard USB-to-UART bridges use dedicated control lines (`DTR` and `RTS`) for serial handshaking. Silicon Labs' **CP2102N** features configurable multi-function GPIO pins with integrated, programmable non-volatile memory (NVM/EEPROM). You can reprogram these pins directly into general-purpose digital outputs that are controlled via USB requests.\n\n---\n\n### Hardware Topology\n\nInstead of passing modem signals through active low-side transistor switches, you connect the CP2102N GPIO pins directly to the target microcontroller's control pins:\n\n```\n+-------------------+                      +-------------------+\n|     CP2102N       |                      |     Target MCU    |\n|                   |                      |   (e.g., STM32)   |\n|  GPIO.0 (Pin 19)  |--------------------->| NRST (Reset)      |\n|  GPIO.1 (Pin 18)  |--------------------->| BOOT0 (DFU Mode)  |\n|                   |                      |                   |\n|  TXD / RXD        |<====================>| RXD / TXD         |\n|  GND              |--------------------->| GND               |\n+-------------------+                      +-------------------+\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Configuration via Simplicity Studio\n\nSilicon Labs provides **Xpress Configurator** inside Simplicity Studio to customize the internal memory of the CP2102N without writing specialized firmware.\n\n1. **Connect the Board:** Connect your CP2102N circuit to your computer via USB.\n2. **Launch Xpress Configurator:** Open Simplicity Studio, detect the CP2102N, and open the **Xpress Configurator** tool.\n3. **Configure Pin Modes:**\n* Locate `GPIO.0` and set its **Pin Mode** to **GPIO (Push-Pull Output)**.\n* Set its **Reset State** / **Initial State** to **High** ($3.3\\text{ V}$) so the target MCU stays out of reset during normal boot.\n* Locate `GPIO.1` and set its **Pin Mode** to **GPIO (Push-Pull Output)**.\n* Set its **Initial State** to **Low** ($0\\text{ V}$) so the MCU defaults to booting from internal Flash memory.\n\n\n4. **Program Configuration:** Click **Program Device** in Simplicity Studio to burn this configuration to the CP2102N NVM.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Controlling the GPIOs in Host Software\n\nBecause these pins are now configured as software-controlled digital outputs, standard serial terminal applications toggling `DTR`/`RTS` won't automatically manipulate them. You control them using Silicon Labs' **CP210xRuntime DLL / API** (or native USB control transfers) via a script or desktop application.\n\n#### Python Script Example (using PyUSB or native `CP210xRuntime.dll`)\n\nUsing Silicon Labs' runtime API (or a library like `pyserial` with vendor control requests), you can execute the exact hardware reset sequence required to enter the bootloader:\n\n```python\nimport time\nimport ctypes\n\n# Load Silicon Labs CP210x Runtime Library\ncp210x = ctypes.CDLL(\"CP210xRuntime.dll\")\n\n# Open device handle\ndevice_handle = ctypes.c_void_p()\ncp210x.CP210x_Open(0, ctypes.byref(device_handle))\n\ndef set_gpios(gpio0_nrst, gpio1_boot0):\n    # Mask: Bit 0 = GPIO.0, Bit 1 = GPIO.1\n    # State: Bit value for each output High(1) or Low(0)\n    mask = 0x03\n    state = (gpio1_boot0 << 1) | (gpio0_nrst << 0)\n    cp210x.CP210x_WriteLatch(device_handle, state, mask)\n\n# --- 1. Enter Bootloader Sequence ---\nprint(\"Setting BOOT0 High, pulling NRST Low...\")\nset_gpios(gpio0_nrst=0, gpio1_boot0=1) # Target held in RESET with BOOT0 asserted\ntime.sleep(0.05)\n\nprint(\"Releasing NRST...\")\nset_gpios(gpio0_nrst=1, gpio1_boot0=1) # Target released from RESET into DFU mode\ntime.sleep(0.1)\n\n# --- 2. Flashing Process ---\n# Execute stm32flash or STM32CubeProgrammer CLI over UART here\n# ...\n\n# --- 3. Normal Boot Sequence ---\nprint(\"Setting BOOT0 Low, pulsing NRST...\")\nset_gpios(gpio0_nrst=0, gpio1_boot0=0) # Reset with BOOT0 low\ntime.sleep(0.05)\nset_gpios(gpio0_nrst=1, gpio1_boot0=0) # Normal execution from Flash\n\ncp210x.CP210x_Close(device_handle)\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### Key Advantages & Considerations\n\n* **Zero Added BOM:** No extra transistors, base resistors, or dual-inverter ICs are needed on the board.\n* **Clean 3.3V CMOS Signals:** Active push-pull outputs deliver sharp edge transitions on `NRST` and `BOOT0` without passive pull-up delays or diode drop issues.\n* **Production Line Programming:** Silicon Labs allows you to export the `.bgf` configuration file generated by Simplicity Studio. For mass manufacturing, programmers or custom scripts can automatically program the CP2102N NVM during initial factory testing using the `CP210xManufacturing.dll` tool."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
