	(text_box "HARDWARE CONSIDERATIONS:\n- SKY13414-485LF Control Pins run on 1.8V Logic /with my MCU on 3.3V I/O\n  - Voltage divider set up on the control pins 1k/1k2\n\n- STM32WL55 PC15-OSC32_OUT pin connected to NX3L1T3_RFVCTRL_S to control the RF peripheral power source.\n\nFor the SKY13414-485LF, adding a full series R + parallel C network to every single logic control pin (V1,V2,V3) is generally not recommended or necessary unless you are solving a very specific, verified noise problem.\nThe datasheet explicitly notes: \"No external components are required for proper operation. DC decoupling capacitors may be added on the VDD and control lines if necessary.\"\nHere is an engineering breakdown of why you should skip the parallel capacitors, how to handle the series resistors, and how to protect your layout.\n\n1. The Danger of Parallel Capacitors (C) on Control Lines\nAdding a capacitor directly from a logic input pin to ground creates a low-pass filter with the trace/driver impedance. While this sounds good for removing high-frequency noise, it causes two major problems here:\nDistorting the Internal Charge Pump: The SKY13414 features an internal negative voltage generator (charge pump) to bias its internal RF FETs. If you place a capacitor on the logic line, high-frequency transients or switching currents from this internal generator can inject noise back into the capacitor, occasionally causing instability or state-glitches.\nDegrading Edge Rates: A parallel capacitor rounds off the sharp edges of your logic transitions. If the digital signal spends too much time in the indeterminate \"middle\" logic region, it can cause the internal decoder to chatter, increasing your switching time (t \nON≈2.2 μs) or generating unwanted RF harmonics during transitions.\n\n2. When to Use a Series Resistor (R)\nInstead of an RC filter, a pure series resistor (placed close to the host microcontroller/driver, not the RF switch) is highly beneficial.\nWhy use it: It limits the di/dt (slew rate) of the digital edge leaving your microcontroller. This stops the control traces from acting as tiny antennas that radiate high-frequency digital noise directly into nearby sensitive RF paths (like the ANT or RF1--RF4 lines).\nRecommended Value: A standard 100 Ω to 470 Ω resistor works perfectly. Because the SKY13414's control input current is practically negligible (I \nCTL≤1 μA), a 470 Ω resistor will only cause a tiny, imperceptible DC voltage drop (∼0.47 mV), keeping your logic thresholds entirely safe.\nBest-Practice Layout Strategy for the SKY13414-485LF\nIf you want to ensure a bulletproof RF and digital interface layout, follow these rules instead:\nVDD Decoupling is Mandatory: While the control pins don't want capacitors, the VDD pin (Pin 4) absolutely does. Place a 10 pF to 100 pF high-frequency ceramic capacitor (0402 or 0201 size) as physically close to the VDD pad as humanly possible to filter out the internal negative voltage generator's switching noise.\nTrace Shielding: Keep the V1, V2, and V3 digital control traces grouped together, and route them away from your RF transmission lines. If they must cross an RF trace, ensure they cross at a strict 90∘ angle on a separate layer with a solid ground plane sandwiched between them.\n\nVia Stitching: Ensure the exposed center paddle of the QFN-14 package is thoroughly stitched directly down into your main system ground plane with multiple microvias to maximize isolation performance (>31 dB @ 2.7 GHz)."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
